assign='''Before counts, form is fixed as one signed plenary merits opinion with a common legal-rule part and a five-Justice application and disposition. The four-Justice disagreement over whether the record permits that application requires a substantive dissent, not fragmentation of the governing rule or multiple lead authors.

'''+assignment(date,eligible,"O'Connor",
'''Fit governs. O'Connor's Coalition separate opinion and Romer join identify the precise distinction between neutral subject-matter centralization and a racially selective barrier. Her frozen commitment expressly applies Coalition's controlling rule and rejects a prior-remediation or hostility prerequisite, so she can state that correction without using her earlier disagreement to alter current law. Kennedy is the strongest alternative at two counted leads, compared with O'Connor's one through Romer and his Coalition join; O'Connor's own prior explanation more directly organizes the subject-versus-race question presented here. Stone-Zsela supplies the fifth application vote and could author, but his agreement alone offers no superior drafting or coalition advantage. No persuasion or new join is inferred from the close vote. O'Connor and Thomas each have one counted opinion; Stone-Zsela, Scalia and Kennedy each have two. All five remain in the pool with a ceiling of two.''')
kernel='''The Court reviews the Ninth Circuit judgment at 307 F.3d 1036, No. 01-15219, affirming judgment for California after a bench trial on Proposition 227's facial equal-protection claim. The October 7, 2002 panel decision and primary text in `runtime/chunk2_a_sources/valeria_lower.txt` supply the trial findings, actual waiver conditions, amendment routes and both strands of the lower reasoning. The operative second amended complaint retained only equality; independent statutory claims were omitted rather than decided by this grant. The inventory supplies the November 25 merits submission. No finding about discriminatory motive, pedagogy or individual educational need is newly made.

Entering law comprises Hunter, Seattle, Crawford, Coalition for Economic Equity, the actual Adarand substantial-justification standard and Romer's bounded equality holding. Coalition displaced a material premise of the earlier circuit approach: a voluntary racial measure is not excluded merely because it did not remedy an adjudicated violation. All Associates accept that actual rule and retain their four/four split about whether the completed record independently establishes a nonracial instructional allocation. The trial finding about pedagogical purpose and the separate political-mechanism question retain distinct roles. Earlier chunk-two holdings do not alter those rules.

Stone's approved core adds the fifth affirmance vote on the concrete absence of racial classification, deliberate racial proxy or racially selective obstruction established here. He rejects a categorical prior-remediation prerequisite and an animus-only test, and supports the independent subject-versus-race determination. He joins the entire Court opinion, including the actual governing equality standard, application and confinement to the facial claim. No standing fallback, new factual premise or substantive narrowing supplies his vote. The four remand commitments remain unchanged; they cannot be counted as application joins merely because they accept Part I.

Continuity: the facial political-process judgment concludes with Proposition 227 effective against this claim. Independent educational and language-access duties retain their own statutory operation; no omitted cause is revived. Breyer's dissent, joined by Stevens, Souter and Ginsburg, preserves their view that the lower court's faulty legal prerequisites prevent a completed structural determination and warrant bounded remand, without finding a violation. No roster, allotment or established external event changes.'''
blocks={
'Event':'''Valeria v. Davis; Ninth Circuit No. 01-15219; judgment below, 307 F.3d 1036. Merits decision, November 25, 2003, on merits submission. The Court reviews the rejection after a bench trial of the facial claim that Proposition 227 racially restructures access to educational policymaking by relocating bilingual-instruction authority from local institutions to the statewide process. The trial court found no discriminatory purpose in the pedagogical choice. Plaintiffs seek relief from the political restructuring; California seeks affirmance.''',
'Participation':'''Stone-Zsela, Stevens, O'Connor, Scalia, Kennedy, Souter, Thomas, Ginsburg and Breyer participate at submission and decision.''',
'Public Action':'''The judgment rejecting the facial equal-protection claim is affirmed.''',
'Judgment & Remedy':'''**Affirmed, 5–4.**

**Supporting:** Stone-Zsela, O'Connor, Scalia, Kennedy and Thomas.

**Opposing:** Stevens, Souter, Ginsburg and Breyer.

Judgment for California stands on the facial political-process claim, and the requested injunction on that theory is denied. Proposition 227 remains effective. The four dissenters would vacate and remand for the distinct structural inquiry rather than immediately invalidate the initiative.''',
'Opinion Topology':'''O'CONNOR, J., delivered the opinion of the Court, in which STONE-ZSELA, C. J., and SCALIA, KENNEDY, and THOMAS, JJ., joined. STEVENS, SOUTER, GINSBURG, and BREYER, JJ., joined Part I. BREYER, J., filed an opinion dissenting in part, in which STEVENS, SOUTER, and GINSBURG, JJ., joined.

| Writing or part | Author | Joins besides author | Scope |
|---|---|---|---|
| Lead Part I | O'Connor | Stone-Zsela, Stevens, Scalia, Kennedy, Souter, Thomas, Ginsburg, Breyer | Governing racial political-process rule and distinction from neutral statewide policy |
| Lead Part II | O'Connor | Stone-Zsela, Scalia, Kennedy, Thomas | Record-specific application and affirmance |
| Partial dissent | Breyer | Stevens, Souter, Ginsburg | Accepts Part I but would vacate for a proper structural application |''',
'Holdings':'''### I. A racial political barrier requires its own inquiry

**Controlling proposition:** A statewide educational-policy change does not violate equal protection merely because the displaced program predominantly benefits racial minorities. A claimant must establish a racial classification, a deliberate racial proxy or racially selective obstruction of ordinary political participation; a prior adjudication of discrimination is not a prerequisite, and lack of racial hostility does not conclusively answer an established racial structure.

**Authority:** Part I of O'Connor's opinion, joined by all nine.

**Controlling explanation:** Hunter and Seattle protect political access against decisionmaking allocations defined by the racial nature of the demand. Coalition for Economic Equity applies that protection to supported selective relocation of authority over otherwise lawful racial measures; calling the measure voluntary or a preference cannot end the inquiry. Crawford preserves neutral policy withdrawal and ordinary allocation of statewide educational authority. These decisions protect equal access, not victory on a preferred policy or an entitlement to every political level.

Demographic concentration and campaign references may bear on the mechanism, but do not alone establish race as the allocation criterion. Language proficiency is not automatically race; a deliberate proxy requires support. Conversely, an actual racial decisionmaking barrier need not be motivated by hostility. Under Adarand, an established racial mechanism requires an important lawful objective, an evidence-supported substantial relationship, justified coverage and actual burdens, and a justified response to concrete practicable alternatives comparably advancing the objective with materially less racial disadvantage. Educational importance alone would not complete that showing. Romer's class-wide exclusion from ordinary protection remains distinct from a neutral curricular choice. The absence of a prior remedial decree and the absence of animus therefore cannot operate as the categorical prerequisites used in portions of the lower court's reasoning.

### II. This record establishes a linguistic instructional policy rather than a racial allocation

**Controlling proposition:** On the supported trial findings, text and operation presented here, Proposition 227's statewide instructional rule does not establish the required racial allocation of political authority. Judgment for California on this facial political-process claim is affirmed.

**Authority:** Part II, joined by O'Connor, Stone-Zsela, Scalia, Kennedy and Thomas; five direct joins.

**Controlling explanation:** The initiative selects the method of teaching children with limited English proficiency, applying the same policy to every family seeking that method. Structured English immersion is normally intended as a transition not exceeding one year, followed by mainstream instruction upon proficiency. Every waiver requires parental consent. Waivers are available when the child already knows English; when a child at least ten would, in the school's judgment, receive better English education through an alternative; or after thirty days of immersion when the school finds special physical, emotional, psychological or educational needs and better educational development through an alternative. Twenty waivers in a grade at a school require an alternative class. Amendment requires electorate approval or a purpose-furthering statute passed by two-thirds of each house and signed by the Governor. These are real restrictions, not unlimited local discretion, but their operative criteria concern instruction and need rather than ancestry.

Plaintiffs and the dissent emphasize that bilingual education overwhelmingly serves minority pupils, campaign materials identified Latino children, and other educational choices remain locally available. Those facts establish the concentrated effect and selected subject of centralization; on this tried record they do not establish that race generated the authority rule. The supported finding concerning pedagogical purpose reinforces, but does not replace, examination of the actual political mechanism. The lower opinion's independently supportable subject-versus-race determination permits final judgment despite its erroneous categorical prerequisites. Coalition concerned expressly race-selected authority; this supported proficiency-based allocation does not present that mechanism. Another inquiry is therefore unnecessary to resolve the facial claim.''',
'Precedent Treatment':'''- Hunter v. Erickson: applied to racially selective political burdens without requiring entitlement to the underlying policy.
- Washington v. Seattle School District: applied to the racial nature of an authority allocation; protection is not confined to programs remedying a previously adjudicated violation.
- Coalition for Economic Equity v. Wilson: retained and applied against categorical exclusion of voluntary lawful racial measures; distinguished in the present application because the supported allocation concerns linguistic instruction rather than race-selected authority.
- Crawford v. Board of Education: applied to neutral policy withdrawal and statewide educational authority while preserving the distinct structural inquiry.
- Adarand Constructors, Inc. v. Peña: its common substantial-justification standard remains the governing standard for an actual racial classification; this record fails to establish the required trigger.
- Romer v. Evans: preserved at its class-wide exclusion-from-protection scope; it does not independently invalidate every subject-specific statewide policy.''',
'Law After Decision':'''Political-process protection requires proof of a racial decisionmaking mechanism and does not depend on a prior remedial decree or hostility alone. Neutral centralization of an instructional subject remains permissible even when its effects concentrate on minority families. Proposition 227 survives this facial equality claim on the supported absence of the required racial mechanism; independent federal educational and language-access duties retain their own force.''',
'Separate Writings':'''Breyer dissents in part, joined by Stevens, Souter and Ginsburg. They join the Court's governing legal rule but would vacate and remand because the lower court repeatedly treated absence of a prior discrimination remedy and racial animus as decisive. In their view its additional nonracial conclusion remains intertwined with those erroneous prerequisites, rather than independently applying Coalition's structural inquiry. Selective removal of bilingual-instruction authority, ordinary local control over comparable educational choices, the overwhelmingly minority population affected and the campaign record present a concrete question whether racial considerations defined the political allocation.

Their remand would examine the text and practical allocation, comparable decisions, context, waivers and amendment burdens on the developed record, respecting existing findings within their actual scope. It would not prescribe a new mandatory checklist or presume discrimination from demographics. If no racial mechanism is established, the State prevails. If one is established, the State must meet Adarand's common standard, and relief must address the proved unequal political barrier under ordinary remedial and state severability rules. The initiative would remain operative absent independently justified interim relief. They would neither direct a preferred curriculum nor declare the initiative unconstitutional on the present record.''',
'Procedure After Action':'''Judgment for California remains in place and the facial political-process claim concludes. The independent statutory claims omitted from the operative complaint are outside this judgment; any independently live claim must be identified and adjudicated under its own governing law.''',
'Source Notes':'''Valeria v. Davis, 307 F.3d 1036, Ninth Circuit No. 01-15219; California Education Code §§305, 310, 311 and 335. The second amended complaint retained the equal-protection claim. The established pedagogical-purpose finding and the asserted structural allocation remain analytically distinct.'''
}
